brian mental health has taken a major  hit since the start of the pandemic  stress and isolation have put many  people in overwhelming positions and  unfortunately  led to a disturbing spike in eating  disorders sasha loftis spoke with one  las vegas woman who struggled over the  last year  and she joins us now live in our  newsroom tonight with her story sasha  denise and brian the woman i spoke with  tonight say she wants those struggling  to know  they're not alone disorders like  anorexia and bulimia are dangerous and  delicate  and they can be brought on by traumatic  events  like covid19 now they can also bring  them back  in full force for those who suffer  it's so easy to negotiate with yourself  and talk yourself through  you know oh i'm just gonna i'm gonna end  this tomorrow courtney olsen shares the  daily struggle of dealing with an eating  disorder  encompasses and takes over your entire  train of thought and your entire  well-being  she's fought symptoms for years and  coveted 19 has brought on an entirely  new battle  from april to august was a super  difficult time i could see myself  becoming unhealthy in terms of  my thought patterns you know starting to  get back into that old behavior  and she's not alone the national eating  disorders association reported a  70 percent increase in calls for help in  2020  as millions deal with stress and  isolation there's been a certain uptick  in  across the board in all mental health  issues since cobit  and seems to be getting worse the longer  we're staying isolated  local psychiatrist dr timothy yeiter  says if you've fallen back to old  patterns  it's more important now than ever to ask  for help  take time to take care of you that's the  biggest thing i can say and  courtney wants anyone suffering to  remember it's okay  to not be okay if you're struggling  know that this too shall pass  and nothing is permanent  now courtney says that video  conferencing from home and  really staring at yourself on a screen  all day can also lead to  obsession over body image if you've been  struggling with an eating  disorder during this time you can call  the national eating disorders  association hotline at 1-800-931-2237  reporting live in the newsroom tonight  sasha loftis 8 news now
 

